Z||000003||84Bill||04-27-2001||12:26 AM||||Well.... I'll tell you something I've done it 3 times and every time it gets a bit easier. So here is my method on the L4 engine<P>1. SPRAY THE mounting nuts with WD40 you can get to the rear ones from under the car along the frame there are slots near the cradle mount just spray like a mad man all up in there. Remove the center console and disconnect the engine wire harness from the ECM there are 4 connectors 2 on the ECM and 2 that cross over to the cars internal harness. then remove the C clip that secures the weather pak to the bulkhead and push the tab in on the left side of the weather pak and push the weather pack thru the hole.<P>2 there is depending on the year another connector that is connected to the engine harness on 84's the harness doubles back and connects to the other harness with a 6.3 mm screw. in the later year cars the harness runs from the left around the engine and goes up to the right frame on the right side near the battery. remove the dust covers from the connector and then remove the screw then pull the connector away there are 2 seperate halfs of the connector that are kinda locked together but with a little pulling they will come apart.<P>(left side) disconnect the trans cooler hoses shift cable(auto) coolant line on the filler neck, Cruise vaccume line remove the caliper from the knuckle wire it up so it wont stress the brakeline and disconnect ebrake cable<P>(rear center) Disconnect the throttle cable and cruise cables the fuel lines and the heater hose under the dogbone mount. and the dogbone from the engine. Under the car dismantle that damnable e brake there is a small clip where 2 cables come together and there are small spring tabs that heep them from coming apart lift the tabs and push the cables together and it will come out through tthe slot.. the same goes for the adjuster.<P>(right side)<BR>disconnect the coolant line from the tube under the car NOT from the water pump, disconnect the heater hose from the water pump, calipers and undo the E brake cable from the caliper<P>(center firewall) <BR>disconnect the AC hoses <P>3. Tidy up the engine compartment bundle up the wire harness so it doesnt get snagged on anything and tuck in loose cables and hoses generally poke around and look for something we might have missed.<P>4 Jack up the car from the center of the engine cradle lower the car onto 2 2x4s set directly under the cradle. here is the tricky part use a jack and jack up the front of the cradle just enough to take the weight off the front mount bolts and undo them, then do the rear mount bolts. Undo the 8 nuts that hold the top of the strut and vent cover mount plate in place.<P>5 Now its just a matter of getting the car up high enough to clear the engine<P>I have some pics on my site from the last engine removal I did If you have any questions just email me and I'll explain it or post a picture on it.<BR> <A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero</A> <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-27-2001||04:23 AM||||I just pulled a 84 4cylinder out from the top, and the process was similar.  I did not see "remove the decklid" in the directions above.  Here's some other differences when you pull it out from the top:<P>1.  You remove the engine and tranny mounts from the cradle.<P>2.  Pull out the drive axles (remove brake calipers and seperate ball joints from the hub/strut assemblies)<P>3.  Remove exhaust pipe, intake, and exhaust manifolds and alternator (including bracket)<P>4.  disconnect clutch slave cylinder and shift cables from tranny, if std. trans.<P>5.  remove rear tranny mount after lifting engine about 6" with the hoist.  This will allow the engine and tranny to come out without jamming in the engine bay (gets a little narrower as you go up)<P>It sounds like a lot of work, probably becuase it is.  But, I was pulling a donor engine from a Michigan car and did not want to stuggle with the cradle bolts.  It was a good decision because I only broke one exhaust manifold bolt.  The engine/tranny mount bolts to the cradle came off with surprisingly little effort.<P>The out-the-top way is probably only an option with 4 cylinder cars, but I have never tried so don't take my word for it.  It was good for me since I have an engine<BR>hoist and a short travel jack.  I did not have to lift the car more than about 18"<P><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000005||KRMFiero||04-27-2001||06:55 AM||||I barly used my chilltions manual, just take the hood off and start disconnecting the wires and cables then lower it down and release the 4 bolts... you will pretty much see every thing you have to disconnect, i did it by myself and i am 15 years old  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||04-27-2001||09:16 AM||||Obviously, everyone here has their own method. I've pulled my motor out twice, and lowered it once for a flywheel swap. My personal opinion is that dropping it is the simplest method. You don't have to yank the axles to do it (provided you aren't changing the transmission)<BR>Whatever method you use to pull it is personal preference. However, when I did mine, I stripped the motor as much as I could while still in the car. Especially intake and valve covers, etc. so I wouldn't have to raise the car any more than necessary. Also, if you do remove it from below, I suggest making a small dolly for the cradle. Use at least 2" casters with rubber wheels (personal preference)and 2x4's. Make the dolly so it is as close to the floor as possible, so as not to add alot of height to the engine. If you're interested, let me know and I'll draw up a diagram on how I made mine, it's only about 3" high and works great. Also, if you can set up the work space so you can roll the engine and trans out from the side at the wheel well, you also save some lifting height. Also, my preference is to remove the struts from the spindles. I just use paint to mark the location, so I can put it back in it's original location.<BR>Good luck<BR>~Taiji<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
